--TEST--
a `type` alias written in a method body, a block or a closure body is `E0233` naming the alias, the same refusal a nested class gets — `rule:types/type-alias`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/type-alias`: `type` is a file- and namespace-scope declaration
// beside `use` and `namespace`, and a class-body member. A body is neither.
// PHP would declare a nested name when the statement *runs*, so whether it
// exists would depend on control flow; Novis resolves every type name against
// a table built before any code runs, so the declaration is refused where it
// is written and named as a `type`, not reported as a statement the grammar
// does not have.
class Outer {
    public static function run(): int {
        type InAMethodBody = int;
        return 1;
    }
}

// A block at file scope is not file scope: the alias would exist only on the
// branch that reached it.
if (true) {
    type InABlock = int;
}

// A closure body is a body like any other.
callable $f = fn (): int => {
    type InAClosure = int;
    return 1;
};

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0233]: a nested `type` declaration is not supported
   --> case.nvs:11:14
%A
error[E0233]: a nested `type` declaration is not supported
   --> case.nvs:19:10
%A
error[E0233]: a nested `type` declaration is not supported
   --> case.nvs:24:10
%A
